## Example PexAssumeUnderTest Attribute
The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is a special attribute used in unit testing frameworks to mark a method as a subject under test. When generating fuzz tests, the methods marked with the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute are often considered as more interesting target for exploration rather than the full code coverage.


For example, consider a class that has a method 'Divide'. We do not want to generate tests for all possible inputs, so we apply [PexAssumeUnderTest]. This causes the method Divide to be considered as a target to generate fuzz tests against.

```csharp
    public class Calculator
    {
        [PexMethodUnderTest] // Mark the method under test
        public double Divide(double a, double b)
        {
            // Implementation left as an exercise
        }
    }
```


Then, in a [PexClass] test class, we can execute fuzz tests for the Divide method.

```csharp
    [TestClass]
    public class CalculatorTest
    {
        [PexClass(typeof(Calculator))]
        public class CalculatorTest

        [TestMethod]
        public void FuzzTestDivide([PexAssumeUnderTest] Calculator calc, double a, double b)
        {
            // Fuzz test code here
        }
    }
```
## Example PexObserve
Sometimes, observing precondition or post condition along the path of your code execution is very important. For instance, observe what is the return value of your method and its behavior. PexObserve is used for this purpose.


Here is a PexObserve example:

```csharp
public class Calculator
{
     public int Add(int a, int b)
     {
         PexObserve("a = {0}, b = {1}", a, b); // This line will observe the input of the method
         int result = a + b;
         PexObserve("result = {0}", result); //This line will observe the result of the method
         return result;
     }
}
```

The above code will generate fuzz tests and will observe the input values a and b, and will observe the result after the addition operation. This provides crucial insights that could be useful while debugging your fuzz tests.

Please note that you need to use pex4mstest to run the fuzz test with PexObserve.


```csharp
[PexClass(ActionUnderTest = typeof(Calculator))]
public class CalculatorTest
{
    [TestMethod]
    public void FuzzTestAdd([PexAssumeUnderTest] Calculator calc, int a, int b)
    {
        int result = calc.Add(a, b);
        // Add a conditional breakpoint based on the result.
    }
}
``` 

This can be done using condition based breakpoint where we add breakpoints based on result. This way, any breakpoint added will only trigger when the condition is met.


## Example PexInstrumentationProvider
PexInstrumentationProvider can be used to provide custom instrumentation for your code under test. The Pex engine uses the PexInstrumentationProvider to gather additional information about your code, which can then be used for better testing.


Here is an example of how you would create a custom instrumentation provider:

```csharp
    // CustomInstrumentationProvider for MyObject and MyMethod
    [PexInstrumentationProvider(typeof(MyObject), "MyMethod")]
    public class MyCustomInstrumentationProvider: PexInstrumentationProvider<MyObject>
    {
        protected override bool CanInstrumentMethod(MethodBase method)
        {
            return method.Name == "MyMethod";
        }

        public override void Preprocess(PexMethodUnderTest method, PexRuntime runtime)
        {
            // ... preprocess instrumentation here ...
        }

        public override void Postprocess(P--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  if new array contains x and old array contains x then old index of x in old array is equal to new index of x in new array
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX && (Old_arrListContainsX ? Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX : true));

            if (Old_arrListContainsX)
            {
	        // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that index of x in old array is equal to index or -1 to avoid failure of test with LastIndexOf(x)
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX || New_arrListLastIndexOfX < Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

