The test session run for 27 seconds. 200 tests were executed, and all of them passed.

## <a name="conclusion"></a>Conclusion

In this lab, we explored how to test an ArrayList and performed manual stress testing to exercise the critical contracts of an ArrayList. While the ArrayList class typically has a robust contract based on the Single Responsibility Principle, the contract of the ArrayList.Remove method is a little more complex, requiring more investigation into the interactions of multiple responsibilities. We found that the contract doesn't always ensure appropriate behavior, and even after analyzing the test results, there could be differences in behavior across different executions of the test.

## <a name="exercise"></a>Exercise

If you have time, let's try implementing our stress tests with Pex to generate a test suite for the ArrayList. This process may provide additional insights into the contract behavior of the ArrayList. How about we try running the generated tests and see what we discover?

```bash
// In your IDE
// Run the stress test

// At a console
cd StressTest
dotnet test
```

Once again, remember that the main goal here is not to get the 100% code coverage, but to demonstrate a manual way of testing an ArrayList by considering multiple responsibilities in a class. In practice, for most testing scenarios, automated tests or tools would be a better solution as they can handle complex scenarios that a human tester could overlook when creating a manual test plan.

This should provide you with a better comprehension of how the ArrayList works and how the contract of an ArrayList.Remove method works.

## <a name="more-information"></a>More information

Take a look at this article which gives a good [summary of how to use Pex](https://docs.microsoft.com/visualstudio/test/using-microsoft-pex-to-find-deterministic-data-ranges-in-unit-tests?view=vs-2019) for testing.
```csharp
[TestClass]
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [TestMethod]
    public void PUT_RemoveContract()
    {
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
        int x = -1;

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", arrList.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", arrList.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", x);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", arrList.IndexOf(x));
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", arrList.IndexOf(x));
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", arrList.Contains(x));
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", arrList.Contains(x));

        arrList.Remove(x);
--------------------            // also, @@@ if arrList contains x then index of x in New arrList should be greater than index of x in Old arrList
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == (New_arrListIndexOfX > Old_arrListIndexOfX));
            
        }
    }
}
```

## <a name="conclusions"></a>Conclusions

The main conclusion of this work is that the PEX framework helps in writing more precise unit tests because it leverages the concept of symbolic execution in the verification of pre/post condition assertions within the unit tests.

It is observed from the generated test cases, that not only is the code coverage maximized due to the effective usage of PEX in the contract testing, but the assertions get valid symbolic values allowing the test framework to work more effectively thus providing a more comprehensive feedback on whether the contract is fulfilled or not.

In this particular example, we wrote a test for `ArrayList.Remove(int x)` method with the contracts for the pre and the post condition checks. Though the actual assertions may need more elaborations, here we have shown a pattern of how the PEX test contracts can be written by focusing on the input parameters and the output parameters.

The PEX-generated unit tests are not only valuable for verifying the functionality of the code but also serve as a useful guide for the developers to understand the expected behaviors and inputs of their code under test, thereby making the maintenance, debugging, and evolution of the code much easier in terms of logic and flow.

Overall, PEX can significantly enhance the software testing process by taking the concept of symbolc execution a step further into software testing by generating meaningful test cases out of the program units and can be a helpful tool for effectively testing complex pre/post condition methods.

