|  **Parameters:**  

* \[ParamType\]int index

|   	|   	|
|---	|---	|
|  **Description:**  Tests RemoveAt(int) 

|   	|   	--------------------            
        }
    }
}
```

As you can see, the contract method has several assertions for us to check if the method really works as expected. The first two lines are our assumptions and we observe what the method does. The last assertion checks if the last index of an item in our original list is smaller or equal than the new length of our list after we removed an item.

Here is the output you should get when running this test:

```bash
   ... PUT_RemoveContract passed
   ... PUT_RemoveContract passed
   ... PUT_RemoveContract passed
```

If everything worked correctly, you should see the same number of passed tests as you have iterations you ran your test under. In our case, we ran it four times - you might run it a few more or a few less times depending on the complexity of your test.

In this test, we're seeing the behavior of `ArrayList` and how it modifies itself when `Remove` is called.

## Conclusion

In conclusion, Code Contracts are a really useful tool when writing tests and can be instrumental in making sure the functionality of your program remains intact. In cases where we are concerned about the object's behavior in a particular scenario, we write a contract for the method and if the method does comply with the contract, then we can be sure it works as expected, else we need to refactor the method. Code Contracts significantly help in reducing the manual efforts of writing unit tests and also ensures the robustness of the program at the same time.

> **Note:** Please install relevant nuget packages in your project(`Microsoft.Contracts.VisualStudio` for Visual Studio, `Microsoft.Contracts.Analyzers` for Visual Studio Code) to use code contracts feature in .NET projects. 

You can learn more about code contracts here: [Code Contracts](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/code-contracts?view=vs-2019). You can also find some great tutorials in the official documentation. Happy Testing =]

